      "output": {
        "message": "Living together makes boundaries trickier because you can't just leave the room and not see them for a week. A few adjustments that help protect your privacy under the same roof: 1. **Pick one \"broken record\" response:** When you share a home, long explanations turn into debates. Pick one neutral line and repeat the exact same words every time they ask: *\"Nothing to report, and I'll tell you if that changes.\"* Then move on or leave the room. If it stays boring, they usually ask less.\n2. **Have the boundary talk when no one has just asked:** Don't wait until they put you on the spot. At a calm, neutral time, say: *\"Since we share a space, I really value having some parts of my life that are just mine. My dating life is one of those things, so I'm not going to discuss it anymore.\"*\n3. **Keep the logistics out of common areas:** Take calls in your room, head out without elaborating on every errand, and keep details sparse. Giving fewer surface clues gives them fewer reasons to interrogate you.",
